README_REASON_NEW = r'''or excluded), the same text as its `explanation` (a payload with no reason keeps the generic one), and, like `insufficient_work_coverage`, a `suggested_objective` of `evidence_cost`;'''

README_LEFT_OLD = r'''reports who was left out as `uncovered_staff_count` and `uncovered_staff_ids`, the page says so in one line, "N of this office's current staff lack the payroll or staffer-roster coverage this model requires and were left out" (a person with dated directory work can be one of them, so the line does not say they have no work records; there is no line in an `insufficient_work_coverage` answer whose origin is the person without coverage: no search ran, so the page does not say that staff were left out), and'''
README_LEFT_NEW = r'''reports who was left out as `uncovered_staff_count` and `uncovered_staff_ids`, and which coverage they lack as `uncovered_basis` (`payroll_or_roster`, or `roster` when `work_sources` is `directory`: that comparison reads no payroll evidence, so payroll covers nobody there and a person with payroll records and no roster entry is left out of it), the page says so in one line, "N of this office's current staff lack the payroll or staffer-roster coverage this model requires and were left out" or, for `roster`, "N of this office's current staff lack the staffer-roster coverage this model requires and were left out" ("lacks ... and was" for one person; an answer with no `uncovered_basis` gets the first wording; a person with dated directory work can be one of them, so the line does not say they have no work records; there is no line in an `insufficient_work_coverage` answer whose origin is the person without coverage: no search ran, so the page does not say that staff were left out), and'''

README_PREWARM_OLD = r'''the milliseconds of the one garbage collection that precedes its first build (`gc_ms`) and the earlier prewarms. When a new router is published the service lets go of the router it replaced at once (its work indexes, hop-row caches and same-displayed-name index; a search still running on it keeps what it holds, one that asks afterwards builds again, and no answer changes), and that collection frees what is left of it before the new indexes are built, so the old router's indexes and the new router's are not resident together. Set `MELANGE_STAFFER_PREWARM=0` before starting the sidecar to turn the prewarm and its collection off; the release at a publish stays.'''
README_PREWARM_NEW = r'''the milliseconds of the one garbage collection that precedes its first build when a router was replaced since the last collection (`gc_ms`; a prewarm that follows no replacement, the first one after a start, collects nothing, because there is nothing to free and a collection holds every request thread while it runs, and has no `gc_ms`, nor has one that is abandoned before it builds) and the earlier prewarms. When a new router is published the service lets go of the router it replaced at once (its work indexes, hop-row caches and same-displayed-name index; the replaced router stores nothing it builds from then on; a request still running on it keeps what it holds, and a request that searches more than once, such as distinct or independent alternatives, keeps the one work index it took at its first pass for all its passes, so a publish between two of them costs no rebuild; a request that asks afterwards builds its own; no answer changes), and the collection that the next prewarm runs frees what is left of it before the new indexes are built, so the old router's indexes and the new router's are not meant to be resident together. That is not guaranteed: a request that was already running on the replaced router keeps its index until it ends, and no collection frees what it still refers to, so for as long as such a request runs the two can be resident together. Set `MELANGE_STAFFER_PREWARM=0` before starting the sidecar to turn the prewarm and its collection off; the release at a publish stays.'''

